<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecuadorian–Peruvian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ecuadorian–Peruvian War was a border war fought in July 1941 between Ecuador and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> over disputed Amazonian and coastal territory. Peru's better-equipped army defeated the Ecuadorians at Zarumilla and occupied the province of El Oro; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio Protocol</a> of 1942 gave Peru most of the disputed land, though the border dispute flared again until 1998.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecuadorian-peruvian-war.jpg" alt="Ecuadorian–Peruvian War" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photos_during_the_Ecuadorian%E2%80%93Peruvian_War.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The dispute dated from before Ecuador's independence, as part of a wider quarrel between Gran Colombia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. It turned on whether Ecuador's territory reached beyond the Andes to the Marañón (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>) river and so included the Amazon basin. Several attempts at settlement failed and produced conflicting treaties, and war came more than once: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-colombia-peru-war">Gran Colombia–Peru War</a> of 1828 and the first Ecuadorian–Peruvian War of 1857 to 1860. The Salomón–Lozano Treaty, signed in secret by Colombia and Peru in March 1922, set their boundary at the Putumayo River and so recognised Peruvian control of much of the disputed region. By December 1940 President Manuel Prado had authorised a Northern Army Detachment, ready for operations by July 1941.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Fighting began on 5 July 1941 on the Zarumilla river, and each side blamed the other for the first shot. Ecuadorian Colonel Luis A. Rodríguez said Peruvian policemen fired on a border patrol; Peruvian accounts said Ecuadorian soldiers from Huaquillas attacked a police patrol at Aguas Verdes. By 6 July Peru was bombing Ecuadorian border posts. Its offensive under General Eloy G. Ureta began on 23 July, with 11,500 to 13,000 men, and overran the post at Chacras the next day. Ecuador had no armour and few aircraft, and Peru's navy and air force had the advantage.</p><p>A ceasefire took effect on 31 July, but that day Peruvian paratroopers captured Puerto Bolívar, and fighting continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, where Second Lieutenant Hugo Ortiz Garcés was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> river on 2 August. By the end of August Peru held El Oro, Loja and Zamora Chinchipe and had begun a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>. The Talara Accord of 2 October ended the clashes and set up a demilitarised zone watched by representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ecuador and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-protocol">Rio Protocol</a> on 29 January 1942, and Ecuador gave up its claim to a sovereign outlet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>. Peruvian troops left El Oro on 12 February 1942. Ecuador withdrew from the boundary commissions in 1948, arguing that the Protocol did not fit the geography, and some 78 kilometres of the border stayed unmarked for fifty years. In 1960 President José María Velasco declared the Protocol void, as signed under occupation, but Peru and four other countries held it valid. The disputes ended only with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenepa-war">Cenepa War</a> of 1995 and the Brasilia Presidential Act of October 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ecuadorian–Peruvian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
